speechtext:
That the Grand Trunk Railway System, which the Government propose to acquire, comprises over eight thousand miles of railway line, owned, controlled or leased and operated by the Grand Trunk Railway Company and its subsidiaries;
That the parent Grand Trunk Railway Company has numerous subsidiary companies, and the system includes, besides the railway mileage herein mentioned, the ownership and management of hotels, steamship lines and other property;
That these railways and other property are partly in Canada, partly in the United States and partly in Great Britain;
That the obligations of these Companies in various forms run into vast sums, and the financial affairs of the several companies are interwoven by guarantees of securities;
That the parent Grand Trunk Company and its chief subsidiary the Grand Trunk Pacific Company are admittedly unable to fulfil their obligations to the Dominion ;
That the information in possession of the House is insufficient to enable the House and the people to fully understand the complicated affairs of the Grand Trunk Railway System;
That there has been no inquiry into the affairs of the Grand Trunk System since that which was conducted by the Drayton-Acworth Commission in the spring of 1917, which related to the Canadian railway situation generally, and incidentally discussed the affairs of the Grand Trunk Railway System ;
That this said Drayton-Acworth Commission in their report say concerning the Grand Trunk Railway Company:
"Even if the Government were to relieve them entirely, as suggested by their President, of their unfortunate Grand Trunk Pacific venture-and, as we have already said, we cannot think that the request can be reasonably justified-it is evident that the Grand Trunk Railway Company is not and will not be for some time to come in a position enabling it to pay out any money at all in dividends. We regard the entire share capital as being intrinsically of but small value at the present time. On the basis of present value of maintainable income the fair compensation would be very small."
That of the stock thus described by the Drayton-Acworth report as of small value the Bill proposes to place a perpetual Government guarantee of interest at 4 per cent on about $60,000,000, and to submit the valuation of about $180,000,000 to arbitration.
That the House is of opinion that before taking further action towards the acquisition of the railways referred to the Government should appoint a Commision composed of persons of recognized ability and experience in railway management, railway finance and railway accounting to make a full inquiry into all the affairs of the Grand Trunk Railway Company and its subsidiary companies, their assets and liabilities, the condition of the railway lines and their equipment, the physical value of the properties, the sums required to put them into efficient condition, their estimated earning powers, and generally into all matters in any way relating to the affairs of the companies herein referred to, and to make a full report thereon for the information of Parliament.
Now, I am sure that hon. gentlemen, listening to that resolution, will feel that Parliament would have been infinitely 
better advised had the wise counsel there proposed been heeded at the time. But what was the position of hon. members opposite? They did everything it was possible to do, within the short space of time permitted, to ridicule this resolution. Everything had to be done in haste, it was contended, the Grand Trunk System must be taken over immediately, and on no consideration could they permit anyone to investigate the affairs of the railways. We find ourselves again in exactly the same position at this session of Parliament. We have been denied any information in regard to the Grand Trunk Railway. When we ask questions we are told that the matter must be referred to a committee, and we are now informed that a committee is at work at present to ascertain what information if any shall be given to this House of Commons.
After two years of bitter experience, and in view of the frightful railway situation that now stares us in the face, to-night, finding ourselves confronted with the question whether we should not still consider possible alternatives to the immediate taking over of the Grand Trunk, the Government says that notwithstanding that it is midnight and that we have had only four or five days in which to consider this problem, we must, before this very night is over, see that this measure is advanced to a stage where, so far as the House of Commons is concerned, the Grand Trunk Railway Company shall immediately become the property of the Government and be placed under its control.
Not only did the Government allow us no information in regard to all that was involved in this tremendous transaction, but when we suggested that the agreement which has given rise to this particular Bill should be submitted to Parliament for its consideration, the Government took again its former attitude in regard to our proposals. On behalf of the Opposition, I moved that the agreement should be subject to ratification by Parliament. That was on November 4, 1919, but that position, which was very reasonable, and which, had it been conceded at the time, might have obviated the necessity for this legislation, was also voted down by the Administration.
I shall not take up the time of the House in reading other amendments.
With regard to the situation which arose in the year following-that is at the session of 1920-we of the Opposition and the House generally, found itself in exactly the same position in regard to the legisla-

tion introduced pertaining to the Grand Trunk. The government found it necessary to bring in another Grand Trunk Bill at that time-a Bill nominally to correct certain errors but in reality to ratify the whole agreement. At that time we of the Opposition asked, respecting the agreement presented to Parliament that we should have an opportunity to consider and to make certain amendments to it, we were told that no amendments would be permitted except the ones that the government itself proposed-that other amendments would not even be considered. So we were denied the right at that time of making any suggestion of an effective kind in regard to this measure.
Mr. Speaker, I have taken the trouble to get the exact figures as to the time which this Parliament has devoted to this allimportant question of the acquisition of the Grand Trunk Railway. I find that so far as this House is concerned that the first reading of the Bill for the acquisition of the Grand Trunk Railway system took place on October 16, 1919. This resolution on which the Bill was founded was introduced on October 15, 1919, and the measure was given a first reading the following day. The second reading took place on October 23, and the third on November 5-in all simply twenty days, including the Saturdays and Sundays which would be involved in that time. Twenty days for a decision by this House on all that is involved in the taking over and acquiring of the Grand Trunk Railway system! Then when the Bill to confirm the agreement, dated March 8, was presented to Parliament in 1920, the first reading was on April 12, the second reading on April 16, and he third reading on April 23-eleven days for the consideration of legislation that involved the further step in the acquisition of this system. That is the way the measure was hurried through Parliament. We come now to what is called the Grand Trunk Arbitration Bill before the House, and which is, as I have said, a measure nominally to ratify certain powers of arbitration but is in reality one to obtain immediate control of the system by forcible means, to use the words of the Prime Minister himself with respect to this acquisition of the Grand Trunk Railway properties. The first reading of the Bill occurred on April 19, the second reading on April 22, and we are now having the third reading on April 25. In other words we are told that beyond six days-notwithstanding that we have heard
from every side that the railway situation is the greatest problem in Canada, that it is the gravest problem that Canada has ever had to consider with the exception of the problem of the war itself-that beyond six days we are not to be allowed any time on which to deliberate on the subject. Mr. Speaker, in view of these facts, it is unnecessary, I think, to go into any argument on the merits of the Bill one way or the other. Where we have inadequate time and insufficient information, I think, in view of the situation as it exists in the country at the present time, the only logical, the only wise, the only prudent step to take is to oppose any legislation which seeks to drive us with further speed, in the dark and without the information we should have, into the particular step which the Government is advocating, and for that reason I intend to oppose the third reading of this Bill.
